    bigIdea: 'Scripture is full of the YOUNG that God called -- and it often tells us their age on purpose. Joseph was "seventeen years old" when his brothers sold him (Genesis 37:2), and "thirty years old when he stood before Pharaoh" (Genesis 41:46) -- thirteen years of forming between the pit and the palace. David was "but a youth" facing Goliath (1 Samuel 17:33). Josiah began to reign at eight and "while he was yet young" -- by sixteen -- "began to seek after the God of David" (2 Chronicles 34:3; 2 Kings 22:1). Jeremiah protested "I am a child" and God answered "Say not, I am a child" (Jeremiah 1:6-7). Timothy had known the Scriptures "from a child" (2 Timothy 3:15). Now the witness of the design: the adolescent brain is wired for intense reward-seeking and strong emotion (the limbic/reward system runs hot) while the judgment-center is still finishing -- which is exactly why God’s answer for the young is TRAINING, not waiting: "Train up a child in the way he should go: and when he is old, he will not depart from it" (Proverbs 22:6). The young are not too unformed to be called; their forming is the very reason to form them well.',
    inApp: 'This week, take the young seriously -- as God does. IF YOU ARE YOUNG: do not let anyone (including your own doubt) despise your youth (1 Timothy 4:12); Josiah sought God at sixteen and Jeremiah was sent as "a child." Give your still-forming judgment the best inputs on purpose -- the Word "from a child" made Timothy "wise unto salvation" (2 Timothy 3:15). IF YOU RAISE OR TEACH THE YOUNG: remember their reward-and-emotion wiring runs strong while judgment finishes last -- so FORM, do not just forbid: train the way (Proverbs 22:6), repeat the Word, model it, and be patient with the gap between what they know and what they do. AND, the guardrail (next lesson): the young are the exact target of the enemy’s strategy precisely because judgment is still building and knowledge is still thin -- so the answer is to fill them with knowledge of their God now.',
