    lesson: 'Read the Scriptures for who God actually CALLS, and you keep meeting the young -- often with their ages named on purpose. Joseph was "seventeen years old" when the brothers who hated him sold him into Egypt (Genesis 37:2); he was "thirty years old when he stood before Pharaoh" (Genesis 41:46). Thirteen years -- the pit, the house of Potiphar, the prison -- and every one of them was forming the man who would save nations. David was "but a youth" when he faced the giant every trained soldier feared (1 Samuel 17:33). Josiah "began to reign" at eight and, "while he was yet young" -- by sixteen -- "began to seek after the God of David his father," and then tore down the idols of a nation (2 Chronicles 34:3; 2 Kings 22:1). Jeremiah tried to beg off -- "Ah, Lord GOD! behold, I cannot speak: for I am a child" -- and God overruled him: "Say not, I am a child: for thou shalt go to all that I shall send thee" (Jeremiah 1:6-7). Timothy had known "the holy scriptures" "from a child" (2 Timothy 3:15), and Paul told him, "Let no man despise thy youth" (1 Timothy 4:12). God is not waiting for the young to be finished before He calls them. Now bring in the witness of the design, carefully. Neuroscience describes the adolescent brain as one where the REWARD system and the EMOTIONAL centers (the limbic system, the amygdala) are highly active and mature relatively EARLY, while the PREFRONTAL cortex -- the brake, the judge, the planner -- is still finishing, into the mid-twenties (this house cites the maturation research, Arain et al. 2013). Practically, that means a young person can genuinely KNOW what is right and still feel an enormous pull toward what is unwise -- the accelerator is strong and the brakes are still being installed. Here is the point, and it is the opposite of what the culture concludes. The culture says: since the brain is unfinished, lower the expectations and wait. God says: since the frame is still forming, FORM IT -- "Train up a child in the way he should go: and when he is old, he will not depart from it" (Proverbs 22:6). The very years the young most feel the pull of folly are the years their frame is most being set -- so those are the years to pour in the Word, the worship, the wise mentors, the modeled walk. Training is not the same as forbidding. Forbidding says "no"; training builds the "yes" underneath it, so that when the judgment-center finishes, it finishes on a foundation of truth. And there is an urgency here that the next lesson will make plain: the young, with judgment still building and knowledge still thin, are the exact target of the adversary’s strategy. The defense is to fill them with the knowledge of their God NOW -- before the enemy fills the gap with his suggestions. (On the timeline: this belongs with the Patriarchs and the kings; it pairs with the Living Lessons "The LORD Looks on the Heart" and "The Flinch Comes First.")',
